Date: Thu, 23 Dec 1993 09:40:27 -0500
From: ddf2@postoffice.mail.cornell.edu (David Friedman)
Subject: Why Have Kids

A number of childless individuals on the list have raised the question of
why one would have children. Since I have three, I thought I would try to
provide an answer. It comes in three parts:

I. The Gene's eye view:

This is obvious. The reason I have children and you do not is that my genes
are better than yours--specifically, better at building a robot that
reproduces them.

II. The Robot's eye view--Children as consumption goods:

Part I raises an obvious question: how do the genes design a robot smart
enough to recognize the possibility of a conflict between the genes'
interest and the robot's interest and do something about it, and still get
the robot to do what the genes want, at least to the extent of having some
children? Part of the answer is by designing the robot to enjoy bringing up
children, thus providing a pleasure that helps balance, in the robot's
calculus, the associated costs. 

I think the best way of describing this phenomenon, for those who have not
directly experienced it, is to say that the experience of interacting with
one's own small children is very much like the experience of being in love.
It lacks the sexual element of being in love, and also lacks much of the
uncertainty and associated discomfort. As with being in love, the object of
the emotion feels intensely important and happiness (and unhappiness) of
the object are mirrored in one's own feelings. 

I suspect that the similarity between the two emotions is more than
coincidence--that "being in love" borrows substantial chunks of code from
the program for "caring for children." My reason for that conjecture is
that some elements of the complex of emotions associated with being in love
seem less appropriate to that context than to the other.

One might, of course, ask why anyone would want to be in love--but I
suspect most readers of this list will not find that particularly puzzling.
Bringing up your own children provides a very similar reward for a much
longer period of time (being in love, unlike loving, seems to characterize
only the initial period of even a successful courtship), and often in a
more satisfactory form. There are, of course, risks--but I think jilted
parents are rarer than jilted lovers.

In addition to this particular strong emotional incentive, there are
others. Bringing up a child, while often boring, frustrating, upsetting, is
also quite an interesting process.

III. Children as Intermediate Goods:

So far I have considered rewards associated with the process of bringing up
children. There are also longer term benefits. One is having the
opportunity to interact with people you like linked to you by a particular
bond--one reinforced by fairly strong genetic programming. You can get
something similar by friendship with people you are not related to, but the
rarity of that kind of friendship is suggested by the fact that "he is like
a son/father to me" is considered a strong statement.

A second benefit is the existence of people--with luck and (your) skill,
competent people--who care a lot about you and will act accordingly. That
was more important in the days before well developed capital markets, when
producing children was probably the best way of providing for your own old
age. But it is still an important form of insurance in a very uncertain
world.

A third benefit is the opportunity to affect the future. If one looks at
what matters to people, I think it is clear that the typical utility
function includes a good deal more than seeking pleasure and avoiding pain,
narrowly defined. One of the additional items is the desire to affect the
world--to discover new ideas, to build a business, to found a family. For
most people, the last of those three is the one in which they have the best
chance of success. And those with the ability to achieve one of the
alternative routes to that objective may also have the genes and the
ability to produce offspring who will also achieve important things.

And then, of course, there is always the use of children as intermediate
goods for producing grandchildren.

"To the authors, by their loving intermediate good" (The inscription on the
copy of _Price Theory: An Intermediate Text_ that I gave my parents).

"Men die, cattle die, but a man's fame lives forever." (Havamal, probably
misquoted, from memory)

      Should Extropians Have Children?

All of these points suggest the question of whether an extropian worldview
provides reasons for or against having children. I think the answer is
both. On the one hand, if you think you are likely to live for hundreds of
thousands of years, you may feel less desire to affect the future
indirectly through your children. On the other hand, if you are going to
live for that long, and your children are too, some of the benefits will
increase greatly. And if the way in which life is extended does not extend
the period during which you can have children--if, for example, we learn to
stop the later stages of aging but not to reverse it--then this is your
only opportunity to have a very interesting experience, and one whose cost,
considered as a fraction of your lifetime resources, is tiny. 

David Friedman
