From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: Freezing Eggs

Kennita Watson:

> Mike Price replied:
>    ...  But if you _did_ have your head (or eggs or almost any
>    tissue) frozen your full genome would be preserved.  Tinkering
>    with the gene control and expression mechanisms (realistically
>    10-30 years, IMO) would enable the culturing of fully viable 
>    ova or whatever.  You'd be keeping your options open about 
>    having children in the future.
> 
> If I'm not mistaken, an egg doesn't contain the mother's full 
> genome; I thought that was part of the point of sexual reproduction.

True, my error.  I was thinking of recovering the original genome from
multiple soma cells (like neurons - that's my Alcor neurosuspension bias
showing, I guess!).  DNA is recovered from mummies and the like by
splicing together broken strands from different cells.  The principle
could be used to recover the full complement from a small sample of ova,
although with more difficulty.

Given N ova (or sperm) from the same individual and perfect recovery
techniques you can recover, statistically speaking, (2^N -1)/2^N of the
genome.  40 ova would be sufficient for total recovery.  From there you
could clone an isolated ovary to harvest as many new ova as desired. 
Well almost.  A young ovary has 100,000s of viable ova - probably
sufficient for all but the most parentally ambitious of us :-)  Of 
course no recovery can be perfect from damaged tissue so extra cells 
are advisable for redundancy.

Women frozen post-menopause would have to recover their genomes from 
soma cells since, as I understand it, no viable ova remain.  No great
problem, IMO, since this seems easier anyway.

