Subject: An idea for insurance

I've thought before on a closely realted subject to what Ms. 
Lebovitz suggested.  That was 'tax insurance'.  You set up
an offshore company that insures you against unpaid taxes.
This could range from taking a small gray-area deduction while
filing with the IRS, all the way to total non-filing.

You provide the tax insurer financial information, and then pay
a premium based on your tax avoidance.  If you are unlucky
enough to get audited, the insurer will compensate you.
For the serious tax-dodger, if you get sent to jail, the
tax insurer pays you a healthy compensation for time spent
behind bars.  Your premium would depend, like other insurance,
on the risk of the bad occurance, whether audit or jail time,
and the amount of compensation you subscribe for (would you
like a $50,000 per year of jail time policy or a $100,00 per
year of jail time policy).

Obviously, this would need to operated off-shore and cryptographically
secure, otherwise the Feds will swoop down and raid the
tax insurer to get their customer list.  By taking the
sting out of the tax-man's actions, it would encourage more
people to avoid paying taxes - which I would call a good thing.

I would rather pay a small premium and know that no matter what,
that questionable deduction will be covered - and the IRS only
can process so many audits - even at today's compliance rates,
they only can audit 1% of returns.

Dani Eder

