Date: Thu, 23 Dec 93 11:19:49 CST
From: eder@hsvaic.hv.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: redundancy

It should be easy enough to work out the duty cycle of the
brain.  Past research should give us the power consumption
of a neuron firing.  We know the top rate of neuron firing
is about 1 kHz.  We know about how many neurons make up
a brain, and the total heat dissipated by a brain (about 25W).

Given this information, you should be able to work out the
average firing rate of neurons, and thus what fraction of
total brain capacity you typically operate on.

Note that it may not be possible to up the duty cycle without
overheating the brain - the relationship between number of
neuron connections to make you smart vs. neuron firing rate
to make yuou think adequately fast (both driven to their
values by evolution), may result in a duty cycle of only 10%
or some other low value, which can't easily be changed
with the current hardware implementation.

Dani Eder

