                 Why We Should _All_ Admire Scrooge
                              
                 by Anthony O'Hear
                 
                 Professor of Philosophy at Bradford University, UK


There are few figures in popular mythology more hated than Scrooge.
Charles Dickens's wizened old miser is the exact opposite of Father
Christmas -- a killjoy, a recluse, a mean-spirited master and intolerant
of children.

His vilification is utterly misplaced.  In my view Scrooge is nothing
less than an heroic figure -- despite all the scorn that has been heaped
upon him and the best efforts of the writer to create a monster.

Scrooge's eternal appeal is that he is the great slayer of humbug.  How
we in Britain today need such a hero.  It was Dickens's genius to create
a popular hate figure who, in fact, has great moral strengths.

In classic social worker fashion, the author even seeks to explain
Scrooge's inhumanity as being due to a loveless childhood.  But the truth
is that in A Christmas Carol, Scrooge displays considerable insight.

He clearly knows, for example, that it is humbug to believe that anything
can be achieved without effort and expense.  He also knows that someone
has to pay for every day a man takes off from work, and that there is
little to be said for making idle people merry at the expense of others.

He refuses to attend parties he would be bored at, just for form's sake.
Doubtless he would have been equally dismissive of any suggestion that he
should send Christmas cards or presents to people he spends the rest of
the year trying to avoid.

As we all know, the personal cost of resisting these little pieces of
humbug can be high.  Unlike Scrooge, most of us are too weak-willed to
pay the price.  But, for me, they are all reasons for believing Scrooge
to be a man of great mettle.

You have only to reflect on what Scrooge is like after his conversion by
the ghosts of Christmas past, present and future to see the force of this.

After he is terrified into repenting of his wicked ways, Scrooge becomes
a frightful bore.  He becomes the life and soul of parties he wouldn't
previously have been seen dead at.  He becomes a busybody questioning
poor beggars in the streets just when they are occupied with trying to
nobble the next man with a few spare coins in his pocket.  He peers into
people's houses and pats little children on the head.

By undermining Scrooge's heroic honesty, the message Dickens seems to 
convey is the message of every politically correct writer and politician
down the ages: that being nice is enough, that social and personal 
problems are largely due to ill will, and that with a pinch of goodwill
on all sides they can be solved.

Ever since Dickens's colossally popular story, those who can't or won't
join in this general humbug are made to feel morally or socially
challenged.  They are made to feel as if they are actually in favour of
oppression and cruelty to the likes of Tiny Tim -- one of the most 
cloying creations in literature.

The truth, of course, is that one can be a resolute member of the Scrooge
anti-humbug party without being wicked or unkind in the slightest.

It is no accident that I celebrate Scrooge's virtues at Christmas.  It is,
sadly, a time when humbug is much in the air.  In Dickensian fashion we
hear endless sermons about Christmas being a time of goodwill as if
mindless niceness was an answer to anything.  But Scrooge is right: this
is all humbug.

Serious problems are not solved by a Christmas card or a drink.  Real
problems usually reflect some deep incompatibility of purpose or tragic
flaw of character or simply the question of too many people striving for
too few things.  Solving them requires the tenacity of purpose and
analytical ability of the unreformed Scrooge.

The lesson most of us take from A Christmas Carol is the very opposite of
that we should derive from it.  Whether Dickens was responsible for this
or whether he was simply reflecting what already existed, humbug is deeply
entrenched in the modern British psyche.  The one dominant trait of
today's Britain is that we all like to believe that we can have desirable
ends without the unpleasant means necessary to secure them.

To take one example, last summer the public opinion polls showed most
people were against having seven-year-old children tested in school.
They also showed that most people believe education is better in France,
despite the fact that France has regular national tests for all children
from the end of their second year.

Nearly everyone said they were concerned about the quality of state
education, but they also believed that the Government's reforms had been
too fast.  So the British public wants a better education system without
the reforms or the tests necessary to secure it.

Similar humbug is much to the fore over Ireland, where it is widely
believed that the problem will just go away if people talk.  Despite
everything that has happened and is still happening, we persist in 
simply ignoring the probably irreconcilable incompatibilities that exist
between the Ulster Protestants and the Nationalists.

Humbug crops up again and again in discussions about foreign policy.  
Thus, from people who know virtually nothing of Balkan history, there
are urgent and persistent calls for us to 'do something' about Bosnia.
No clear indication is given of just what we should do, or how, once in,
we should get ourselves out -- a classic example of sentimental humbug.

Another form of humbug has been over the Government's commitment to
family values.  It says how devoted it is to the institution of the
family, yet in humbuggish fashion, it penalises the family financially
through tax changes and makes divorce easier through legal proposals.

Europe is another bumper humbug arena.  No one I know, even those who are
generally in favour of European integration, likes the Brussels
regulations that we are now more and more constrained by.  Yet politicians
of all sides tell us we have to tolerate them and obey them, despite the
fact that they are widely flouted in other parts of Europe.

Then, as anyone who ever watches Question Time will know, pretty well
everyone in the country wants bottomless public spending on things like
the arts and the National Health Service and yet -- and this is the rub --
they want lower taxes at the same time.

There was a simultaneous outcry recently about the proposal for raising
money by putting VAT on books and about the proposal for saving money by
a general cut in the Arts Council grant.  It was particularly intriguing
because the outcry was largely from the very same people on both sides.

I am not saying this humbug is wicked.  Often, indeed, it stems like most
humbug from too much niceness and too strong a desire not to offend or to
be unkind.

But it does represent a profound flabbiness in our thinking and in our 
psychology.  Britain needs a new Scrooge to restore backbone to the nation.
