Markets will differ and some will be harder than others.  You may also be 
encountering anti-male bias.  It did take my son longer to crack the NYC 
temp market than my daughters even though his typing skills were equal 
(and his computing skills superior -- sorry gals).  Once he got going, he 
had no trouble finding assingments.  

You can try for a receptionist/light typing assignment first rather than 
straight word processing.  Once an agency sees you bringing them money, 
they will be more likely to place you.  It should go without saying but it 
doesn't these days that you should have neat hair and clothes -- 
conservative attire.

The other thing you have to do is keep hitting agencies.  All the 
agencies.  And keep hitting them.  Be methodical.  Go through the yellow 
pages (Employment Agencies - Temporary).  Atlanta may be a problem because 
the jobs are more spread out and they may want people with cars.  Try the 
suburban "Edge City" areas if you have wheels.

Lie about you credentials.  Find people you can claim to have worked for 
who will cover for you.  Do you know anyone who works in any kind of an 
office where they can take phone calls.  Remember anyone in a company 
could have hired a temp and thus could provide a reference.

Good luck.

Duncan Frissell 

